"texte": [
"Standing at the podium during his graduation commencement speech, renowned ethicist Dr. Marcus Thorne posed an unsettling question to the audience: 'How many of the life choices you are celebrating today were truly yours, and how many were quietly scripted by invisible social norms?' A murmur rippled through the auditorium as graduates exchanged glances. 'We pride ourselves on being autonomous individuals,' Thorne continued, 'yet from the careers we pursue to the milestones we chase by age thirty, we constantly conform to unspoken expectations.'",
"Thorne explained that social norms function as an invisible architecture guiding human behavior. On one hand, they maintain social order and foster predictability; without shared rules of etiquette and morality, daily coexistence would descend into chaos. On the other hand, when norms become overly prescriptive, they stifle authenticity and punish deviation. Fear of social ostracism often compels people to suppress their genuine desires in favor of safe, conventional paths.",
"'Consider how powerfully peer pressure operates even in adulthood,' he remarked. 'Even though nobody explicitly forces us to buy a prestigious car or follow a linear career trajectory, subtle cues from media and family reward compliance and ridicule eccentricity. Consequently, many individuals wake up in midlife realizing they have climbed a ladder leaning against the wrong wall.'",
"Concluding his address, Dr. Thorne challenged the graduates to practice conscious non-conformity. 'True freedom does not mean rejecting every rule recklessly,' he advised. 'Rather, it requires interrogating the norms you have inherited so that when you do conform, it is by deliberate choice rather than blind habit.'"
