"When twelve-year-old Leo submitted a short science-fiction story explaining cellular mitosis for his biology assignment, he expected praise for his originality. Instead, his teacher handed the paper back with a low mark and a stern note in red ink: 'Stick to the textbook definitions.' Disheartened, Leo threw his story into the recycling bin and resolved never to deviate from standard formulas again. Unfortunately, Leo's experience is far from isolated; across the globe, education systems routinely treat creativity as a frivolous extracurricular hobby rather than an essential cognitive skill.",
"Historically, school hierarchies have placed mathematics and languages at the pinnacle, while relegating music, visual arts, and drama to the margins. Whenever school budgets tighten, artistic programs are almost invariably the first to be cut. Yet, neuroscientists and economists increasingly warn that marginalizing creativity is profoundly shortsighted in an era of rapid automation.",
"While artificial intelligence excels at routine calculations and data processing, divergent thinking—the capacity to generate original ideas and connect seemingly unrelated concepts—remains uniquely human. Moreover, creativity is not confined to painting or poetry; it is the driving force behind scientific breakthroughs, sustainable engineering, and diplomatic conflict resolution.",
"Therefore, elevating creativity in schools does not mean abandoning academic rigor. On the contrary, when teachers encourage experimentation, tolerate constructive failure, and integrate artistic inquiry across all subjects, students become far more engaged and resilient thinkers. If society hopes to solve unprecedented global challenges, schools must stop educating creativity out of children."
